RemoveTorrent normally reads tc.PID from config.json to reconcile liveness (store.ReconcileLiveness) before deciding whether there's anything to stop. When config.json itself can't be loaded/parsed, that path is gone entirely -- there's no pid to check, so RemoveTorrent can't tell a live orphaned child from a dead one. Leaving such an entry permanently un-removable (the prior behavior: LoadTorrentConfig's error just propagated straight out) wasn't acceptable, but neither is deleting ~/.tocli/torrents/<id> unconditionally -- that risks pulling the directory out from under a process that's still actually running and writing into it.

removeWithUnreadableConfig (RemoveTorrent's new fallback for this case) substitutes the per-torrent advisory lock file for the liveness signal instead: it calls process.AcquireLock on the same lock file engine.Run holds, which needs no readable config at all. If that returns process.ErrLockHeld, something is still running and removeWithUnreadableConfig refuses to delete anything, surfacing a clear error instead. If the lock is free, it's safe to delete, so it releases immediately and calls os.RemoveAll on the whole tracking directory (RemoveOutcome.ConfigUnreadable is set so DescribeRemove can word this outcome distinctly from the normal removal paths).

This does mean 'remove from list only' and 'remove with data' collapse into the same action here -- without a readable config there's no save path to distinguish them by, so there's nothing left for that distinction to mean.
